#N canvas 40 60 1280 1060 12;
#X declare -path ..;
#X obj 0 0 cnv 1 1280 46 empty empty 07\ \ \ Hopfield\ networks\ (1982)\ \ -\ \ a\ memory\ for\ grooves 14 23 0 20 #1d3557 #ffffff 0;
#X obj 1110 1030 declare -path ..;
#X text 800 1030 finds the nn.* objects one folder up ->, f 42;
#X text 20 1030 neuralplay - Moisés Horta Valenzuela / SEMILLA.AI Studio, f 70;
#X text 20 56 A network that REMEMBERS. 32 neurons hold two bars of 16th notes: on = low drum \, off = high drum. Three grooves are stored in the connections. Mess one up \, press recall \, and listen to the network pull it back - one neuron at a time - into the closest memory., f 175;
#X obj 10 96 cnv 1 390 570 empty empty THE\ IDEA 8 12 0 13 #eef1f5 #1d3557 0;
#X text 20 122 Donald Hebb (1949): "neurons that fire together wire together". To STORE a groove \, strengthen the connection between every pair of neurons that agree \, weaken it between pairs that disagree:, f 52;
#X text 30 203 W_ij = W_ij + s_i * s_j / 32 (s = +1 or -1), f 52;
#X text 20 224 To RECALL \, pick a neuron \, let all the others vote through their connections and take the sign: s_i = sign( sum over j of W_ij * s_j )., f 52;
#X text 20 275 John Hopfield (1982) showed that each such update can only lower an ENERGY \, E = -1/2 sum W_ij s_i s_j. Stored grooves sit at the bottom of valleys in this landscape: start nearby and you roll down into them. Memory as physics - Hopfield and Hinton received the 2024 Nobel Prize in physics for it., f 52;
#X text 20 386 LIMITS: about 0.14 x 32 = 4 grooves fit before memories blur into "spurious" mixtures. And every memory has a twin: its negative (low and high swapped) is a valley too., f 52;
#X obj 410 96 cnv 1 860 270 empty empty THE\ GROOVE\ \ (X\ =\ low\ drum\ =\ +1\,\ empty\ =\ high\ drum\ =\ -1).\ Click\ to\ edit\ it. 8 12 0 13 #e3f0fb #1d3557 0;
#X text 420 162 bar 1, f 6;
#X text 420 206 bar 2, f 6;
